      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright© 2017 International Institute of Anticancer Research (Dr. John G. Delinassios), All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The tumescent mastectomy technique has been used to facilitate dissection of subcutaneous tissue and mammary gland in order to reduce intraoperative bleeding and speed the operation."
          },
          {
            "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "A prospective clinical study was performed on 30 female patients undergoing immediate breast reconstructions after skin-sparing mastectomy (SSM) or nipple-sparing mastectomy (NSM) in order to assess early postoperative complications, pain, and final esthetic outcome of skin flaps related to tumescent anesthesia (TA)."
          },
          {
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "TA significantly speeded-up the operative procedure (131±49.99 vs. 180.5±67.15 min; p=0.03) achieving less skin damage compared to patients who did not have TA (p=0.045); moreover, no significant difference occurred with regard to the length of in-hospital stay and overall lymphatic drainage."
          },
          {
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The length of the operation as well as the final cosmetic outcome of skin flaps was significantly improved due to TA, with no appreciable side-effects."
          }
        ]
      },
